    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d619">

                <pb facs="0521" n="509" xml:id="pg509"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/25177: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-10-30T21:00:00+01:00">October 30, 1942—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 30—5:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6069. We took up immediately with Cadogan<anchor n="82" xml:id="d619-f1"/> in
                    Eden’s absence the matter of the report of the official assurances which the
                    British Consul General at Tangier desires to have given to Orgaz (Department’s
                    telegram No. 5381, October 29, 11 a.m.). Cadogan said that there must be some
                    misunderstanding since Gascoigne had been given explicit instructions that he
                    should not encourage Spanish aspirations with respect to French Morocco. He said
                    that point 2 definitely did not represent the views of the British Government
                    and that further instructions to correct the impression would be sent
                    immediately to Gascoigne.</p>
                <p>Sir Alexander said that the view of the British Government is that the question
                    of Spanish aspirations for territorial acquisition at the expense of French
                    Morocco is a matter to be settled between France and Spain at an appropriate
                    time. Meanwhile he said the British Government has no intention of compromising
                    the position of either party.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d619-f1" n="82" xml:id="d619-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Alexander Cadogan, British Permanent Under Secretary of State for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
